; C++ scoping. Functions, lambdas, blocks and for loops open scopes; a declaration binds its
; name in the innermost one, so a use before the declaration sees the outer name (bindings are
; ordered, hoisted_bindings is false). Methods see the enclosing class's fields and methods (class_scope_visible is true).
; Out-of-class definitions (`void A::f() {}`) cannot see the class scope lexically.
;
; Declarators wrap the name (pointer, array, function pointer, reference); each declaring context lists the
; wrappings up to three deep, plus the function-pointer shape. Deeper nesting is not bound.

; not variable references
(qualified_identifier name: (identifier) @ignore)

; scopes
(function_definition) @local.scope
(lambda_expression) @local.scope
(field_declaration_list) @local.scope
(compound_statement) @local.scope
(for_statement) @local.scope
(for_range_loop) @local.scope
(if_statement) @local.scope
(while_statement) @local.scope
(switch_statement) @local.scope
(catch_clause) @local.scope
(preproc_function_def) @local.scope
; the parameter list of a prototype or function pointer: its names are not the enclosing scope's
(declaration declarator: (function_declarator parameters: (parameter_list) @local.scope))
(declaration declarator: (pointer_declarator declarator: (function_declarator parameters: (parameter_list) @local.scope)))
(field_declaration declarator: (function_declarator parameters: (parameter_list) @local.scope))
(field_declaration declarator: (pointer_declarator declarator: (function_declarator parameters: (parameter_list) @local.scope)))
(parameter_declaration declarator: (function_declarator parameters: (parameter_list) @local.scope))

